.TH tapectl-drive-poll 1  "tapectl-drive-poll " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-drive\-poll \- Read the drive\*(Aqs health pages (and the cartridge chip, when one is loaded) and record them — read\-only, through the SCSI generic node only
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl drive poll\fR [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read the drive\*(Aqs health pages (and the cartridge chip, when one is loaded) and record them — read\-only, through the SCSI generic node only
.PP
Sends LOG SENSE, READ ATTRIBUTE and INQUIRY to the backend\*(Aqs `device_sg`, and nothing else: it never opens the tape node, never moves the tape, never loads or ejects a cartridge. Every log page it reads is journalled verbatim as a contact of its own (`report health` shows the reading), so a TapeAlert page the drive clears on read is never lost. With no known cartridge loaded the reading is the drive\*(Aqs alone.
.PP
Takes the drive lock without waiting: while a tapectl command has the drive it reads nothing and exits 75. Exit 0: recorded, the drive reported nothing. Exit 1: recorded, and the drive raised a TapeAlert or reported an unrecovered error (named). Exit 2: no reading — the sg node could not be read, or the drive is not configured. Run daily by `contrib/systemd/tapectl\-drive\-poll.timer`.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
The drive to poll, by its tape node path as configured (`[[backends.lto]].device_tape`; a by\-id link). Defaults to the only configured drive. The tape node itself is never opened
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes. Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr. Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
